            "relevance_3LNXgq": "- **Relevance Score:** 75/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Highly Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses prison\u2011based survey to detail inmates' ages, prior education, and work, examining right to university; lacks mental\u2011health focus and cross\u2011jurisdiction comparison.",
            "abstract_5B4jjq": "En esta tesis se busca reconocer y describir anal\u00edticamente el conjunto de acciones que se desarrollan, y se profundizaron en las \u00faltimas dos d\u00e9cadas, en torno a la modalidad educaci\u00f3n superior en contexto de encierro en el \u00e1mbito de las Universidades p\u00fablicas argentinas y, en particular, se problematiza la experiencia de la Facultad de Periodismo y Comunicaci\u00f3n Social (FPyCS) de la Universidad Nacional de La Plata (UNLP) para promover el derecho a la Universidad de las personas privadas de libertad. En tal sentido, este estudio requiri\u00f3 poner en di\u00e1logo esta experiencia espec\u00edfica en el contexto de las pol\u00edticas nacionales de educaci\u00f3n en \u00e1mbitos de privaci\u00f3n de la libertad en t\u00e9rminos hist\u00f3ricos. De este modo, se pretende trascender la instancia exploratoria y dar cuenta de las decisiones para la inclusi\u00f3n educativa de dichas pol\u00edticas, de las estrategias y acciones que se despliegan en torno a ellas. ",
            "education-lev_LZfD4h": "The PDF indicates that at the time of entry into prison, 5.1% of individuals had incomplete primary education, 10.1% completed primary, 22.8% incomplete secondary, and 43% completed secondary. Only 1.3% had completed university, and 7.6% had incomplete university studies. The age distribution at penal system entry shows 44% were 20-29 years old, 24% were 30-39, and 18.7% were under 20. Pathways to incarceration are not explicitly detailed as poverty, abuse, substance use, or mental illness.",
            "mental-health_Bk3U8f": "Not addressed in this paper.",
            "prisoner-righ_Ulqd1Y": "Prisoners' legal rights to education are enshrined in constitutional, statutory, and human rights frameworks, though often understood as a benefit rather than a right (p. 12, 44, 82-84). Grievance procedures and advocacy mechanisms include judicial writings, *habeas corpus*, and student centers (p. 265). Prisoner councils, like the CEUSTA, act as spaces for organization, self-management, and resistance, promoting autonomy and collective empowerment (p. 253, 261). Barriers to exercising rights include bureaucratic hurdles, arbitrary decisions by penitentiary staff and judiciary, lack of resources, and constant transfers (p. 203, 232, 240, 242). Prisoners want to study, grow as individuals, and secure a dignified future and employment upon release (p. 228, 335-337).",
            "prisoner-righ_UGCvQi": "Not addressed in this paper.",
            "mental-health_MAwBu4": "Not addressed in this paper."
        },
