            "relevance_3LNXgq": "- **Relevance Score:** 72/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses prison mental health care experiences to examine inmate needs, voice, and service gaps; provides direct insights into mental health status and preferences, but lacks education or age\u2011entry analysis and cross\u2011jurisdiction comparison.",
            "abstract_5B4jjq": "",
            "education-lev_LZfD4h": "Prisoners often have poor educational attainment; 65% have numeracy skills and 48% reading skills at or below an 11-year-old level. Many stopped regular school attendance years before the minimum leaving age, with few having higher education. Most offending is by 15-21 year olds. Pathways include poverty, abuse, homelessness, substance misuse, and mental health problems. Many had first contact with police/courts in early teens.",
            "mental-health_Bk3U8f": "Prevalence of mental illness is high, with 66% having personality disorder, 45% neurotic disorder, and 8% schizophrenia (Table 1, p. 14). Suicide risk is five times higher for male prisoners, with key periods being the first seven days and post-release (p. 20). Health care services are often limited, with poor screening (p. 27), rushed consultations (p. 48), and limited access to psychological therapies (p. 50). Barriers include staff shortages, inflexible appointments, and lack of inter-service understanding (p. 8). Many prisoners lack trust in staff for mental health discussions (p. 29) and report unmet needs for \"someone to talk to\" and better release planning (p. 58-59).",
            "prisoner-righ_Ulqd1Y": "Prisoners often felt powerless and had little influence on decisions affecting them. They desired \"someone to talk to\" about their feelings and problems, better release planning, meaningful daytime activity, crisis help, and psychological therapy/medication advice. Many reported mental health services were unhelpful outside prison. Grievance procedures were not explicitly detailed, but prisoners were reluctant to complain about bullying due to fear and perceived lack of serious attention. Inreach teams acted as advocates, helping with release preparation, accommodation, and medication. However, most prisoners lacked access to this support.",
            "prisoner-righ_UGCvQi": "Not addressed in this paper.",
            "mental-health_MAwBu4": "This paper does not provide data specifically on mental health conditions or service delivery in U.S. prisons. The statistics and information presented regarding prevalence rates of mental illness, types of conditions, service availability, treatment gaps, medication access, crisis intervention, suicide rates, and unmet mental health needs are focused on prisons in England and Wales. Therefore, specific data for U.S. prisons is not addressed in this paper."
        },
        {
            "papers_Knp69H": {
                "unique_id": "16jo1t7dwg",
                "id": 3009041085,
                "full_slug": "enhancing-female-prisoners-access-to-education-16jo1t7dwg",
                "title": "Enhancing female prisoners' access to education",
                "abstract": "The rate of female incarceration continues to surge, resulting in over 714,000 women currently being held behind bars worldwide. Females generally enter carceral facilities with low educational profiles, and educational programming inside is rarely a high priority. Access to education is a proven contributor to women\u2019s social and economic empowerment and can minimise some of the obstacles they encounter after being released from custody. Support for the intellectual potential of incarcerated female \u2018students\u2019 can address intersecting inequalities that impede access to social protection, public services and sustainable infrastructure.\u00a0 Policymakers, academics and activists concerned with gender equality must begin by focusing on academic and vocational program development for female prisoners, built through strong community partnerships, and inclusive of trauma informed supports.",
                "is_archived": false,
                "publication_type": "Journal Article",
                "doi": "10.5204/IJCJSD.V9I1.1468",
                "date": "2020-02-01 00:00:00+00:00",
