quities based on personal characteristics such as age, race/ethnicity, citizenship, sexual orientation, gender identity and expression, physical and mental disabilities and substance abuse histories, as well as social structural factors including poverty, unemployment, homelessness, and histories of having lived in unsafe neighborhoods. These multi-dimensional factors have implications for bias or discrimination in employment, which are exacerbated by their incarceration status.The incarcerated population is often classified as 'older adult' or 'elderly' beginning in their 50s, as opposed to the traditional retirement age of 65 (Falter, 2006). This age differentiation is attributed to a process of accelerated aging in which the average prisoner may have a reduced health status comparable to non-incarcerated individuals who are 10 to 15 years older (Reimer, 2008). This process of accelerated aging may be due to prisoners' high-risk personal histories, which may be compounded by the often stressful conditions of confinement (Aday, 2003; Maschi, Kwak, Ko, & Morrissey, 2012). Once released from prison, their health status has significant implications for employment prospects, especially if employers are biased or discriminate against potential employees based on age or disabilities.The population of older incarcerated adults is presently five times as large as it was in 1990 (Sabol & Couture, 2008). This dramatic increase is not the result of an increase in criminal activity among individuals in this age group but rather has been attributed primarily to stricter sentencing policies, such as mandatory minimum sentences, and \"three strikes your out\" laws (ACLU, 2012; Maschi, Morrissey, Immagieron, & Sutfin, 2012). The conservative shift in criminal justice punishment ideology beginning in the 1970s resulted in stricter public and legislative policies and court decisions awarding adjudicated offenders longer mandatory prison sentences, which increased the likelihood that those incarcerated would not only age in prison, but increased the likelihood that they could even die in prison (HRW, 2012). \u2026",
            "education-lev_LZfD4h": "Not available in metadata.",
            "mental-health_Bk3U8f": "Not available in metadata.",
            "prisoner-righ_Ulqd1Y": "Aging people in prison face challenges in satisfying age-specific educational, training, employment, financial, and civic participation rights. The metadata highlights human and civil rights organizations detailing the plight of older adults in prison, including histories of unemployment and poverty. The study suggests comprehensive services providing prison and post-prison release education, vocational training, housing, and job placement for incarcerated and formerly incarcerated older adults to address their economic and employment prospects. Culturally responsive tools and resources are also discussed.",
            "prisoner-righ_UGCvQi": "The study highlights challenges for aging people in prison and post-release in satisfying age-specific educational, training, employment, financial, and civic participation rights. It notes that stricter sentencing policies like mandatory minimums and \"three strikes\" laws have increased the likelihood of aging and dying in prison. The paper discusses human and civil rights organizations (ACLU, HRW) detailing the plight of older adults in prison, implying advocacy for their rights.",
            "mental-health_MAwBu4": "The metadata indicates that older incarcerated adults, often aged 50+, experience accelerated aging, with a health status comparable to non-incarcerated individuals 10 to 15 years older. This accelerated aging process may be compounded by stressful confinement conditions. The text mentions \"physical and mental disabilities and substance abuse histories\" as personal characteristics influencing social inequities. However, specific prevalence rates of mental illness, types of conditions, availability or quality of mental health services, treatment gaps, medication access, crisis intervention, suicide rates, or unmet mental health needs are not detailed."
