            },
            "relevance_3LNXgq": "- **Relevance Score:** 67/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses inmate surveys to map mental\u2011health problems and barriers, directly targeting incarcerated adults; does not address education, age of entry, or cross\u2011jurisdiction comparisons; offers a needs assessment of services and captures inmate perspectives, touching on voice but not broader rights advocacy.",
            "abstract_5B4jjq": "Objectives: Because of increasing numbers of mentally ill offenders without concomitant increases in mental health staffing, issues of service utilization and priority populations have become paramount. The aim of this study was to expand upon previous studies of correctional mental health service utilization by examining the underlying factor structure of problems for which inmates may seek services and by examining the barriers hindering inmates\u2019 willingness to seek services. Additionally, this study investigated whether previous treatment experiences had an impact on inmates\u2019 willingness to seek services or barriers to seeking services. Methods: Participants consisted of 418 incarcerated adult males who voluntarily participated in this study and were from three security levels (that is, reception and diagnostic unit, minimum security, and maximum security). Participants completed a threepage survey inquiring about use of mental health services before and during incarceration. Results: Factor analysis indicated five types of problems for which inmates may request mental health services (behavioral dyscontrol, physical health concerns, negative affect, interpersonal relationships, and institutional relations) and four types of potential barriers to service utilization (self-preservation concerns, procedural concerns, self-reliance, and professional service provider concerns). Inmates with no prior history of mental health service use were more likely to present with self-preservation concerns about services and present as self-reliant. Conclusions: Results of this study provide an overarching conceptualization of barriers hindering inmates\u2019 willingness to seek services, as well as the types of problems for which they are likely to seek services. Also, inmates\u2019 mental health treatment history affected their current perceptions of services. (Psychiatric Services 58:1181\u20131186, 2007)",
            "education-lev_LZfD4h": "Not available in metadata.",
            "mental-health_Bk3U8f": "The study examined needs for and barriers to correctional mental health services. It identified five types of problems for which inmates seek services: behavioral dyscontrol, physical health concerns, negative affect, interpersonal relationships, and institutional relations. Four barriers to service utilization were found: self-preservation concerns, procedural concerns, self-reliance, and professional service provider concerns. Inmates with no prior mental health service history were more likely to exhibit self-preservation concerns and self-reliance regarding services. The study aimed to expand on previous work due to increasing numbers of mentally ill offenders without increased staffing.",
            "prisoner-righ_Ulqd1Y": "The study examined inmate perceptions of problems for which they seek mental health services, including behavioral dyscontrol, physical health concerns, negative affect, interpersonal relationships, and institutional relations. It also investigated barriers to seeking services, which included self-preservation concerns, procedural concerns, self-reliance, and professional service provider concerns. Inmates without prior mental health service use were more likely to exhibit self-preservation concerns and self-reliance regarding services. The research provides a conceptualization of these barriers and the types of problems inmates are likely to seek help for, noting that treatment history influenced perceptions.",
            "prisoner-righ_UGCvQi": "The study examined inmate perceptions of problems for which they seek mental health services and barriers hindering service-seeking. Inmates identified five problem types, including behavioral dyscontrol and interpersonal relationships, and four barrier types, such as self-preservation concerns and self-reliance. The study investigated whether prior treatment experiences impacted willingness to seek services or perceived barriers. No information on specific prisoner rights, grievance systems, legal rights, advocacy mechanisms, complaint processes, respect for dignity, or prisoner councils in U.S. prisons is provided.",
            "mental-health_MAwBu4": "This study examined mental health service utilization among 418 incarcerated adult males in three security levels. It identified five types of problems for which inmates seek services: behavioral dyscontrol, physical health concerns, negative affect, interpersonal relationships, and institutional relations. Four barriers to service utilization were found: self-preservation concerns, procedural concerns, self-reliance, and professional service provider concerns. Inmates without prior mental health service use were more likely to exhibit self-preservation concerns and self-reliance."
        },
