            "relevance_3LNXgq": "- **Relevance Score:** 67/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses prison sample to assess psychiatric treatment needs and ethnic disparities; directly measures mental health status and unmet needs, includes needs assessment, touches on equity but not education or entry age.",
            "abstract_5B4jjq": "To investigate to what extent mental health treatment needs are met in white and black and minority ethnic (BME) prisoners. Treatment needs of a random sample of male (n\u2009=\u2009197) and female (n\u2009=\u2009171) prisoners were assessed with standardised instruments. Interventions provided were confirmed through face-to-face interviews and case note review. BME prisoners comprised 44% of participants. Treatment for depression, personality disorder and alcohol abuse was more often needed by White than BME prisoners. Needs were more frequently met in White than BME prisoners (48.5% vs 38.2%; p\u2009<\u20090.05), largely due to the delivery of psychological treatments. BME prisoners had fewer of their mental health needs met.",
            "education-lev_LZfD4h": "BME prisoners were more likely to have achieved standard school leaver (General Certificate of Secondary Education) or higher educational qualifications than White prisoners (63.2% vs 46.3%; X\u00b2 = 9.7, p = 0.002). This unexpected finding might arise from higher levels of white-collar crime among sampled BME prisoners or a lower general threshold for imprisoning BME individuals. The paper does not provide specific data on literacy rates, age at first arrest/incarceration, juvenile vs. adult entry, or age distribution of the prison population. Pathways to incarceration such as poverty or abuse are not detailed.",
            "mental-health_Bk3U8f": "Prisoners exhibit high rates of psychiatric disorder; 70% had two or more disorders and 11.7% had at least five. Around half of identified mental health needs were unmet. White prisoners needed treatment for depression, personality disorder, and alcohol abuse more often. Overall met need was higher for White prisoners (48.5%) than BME prisoners (38.2%; p<0.05). Needs for psychological treatments were met significantly more often in White prisoners (44.2% vs 26.5%; p<0.05). BME prisoners had significantly fewer mental health needs met. Staff training on recognizing mental health issues in BME prisoners is highlighted as important.",
            "prisoner-righ_Ulqd1Y": "The paper notes that equity requires prisoners to have equal access to care regardless of ethnicity, aligning with the principle that prisoners should receive the same mental health care as in the community. It mentions that prisoners often asked for help with problems, but requests were rejected twice as often in prison. The assessment of needs included service users' attitudes towards treatments. BME prisoners had significantly fewer of their mental health needs met. Language difficulties and cultural factors were identified as potential barriers to identifying mental illness or seeking help.",
            "prisoner-righ_UGCvQi": "Not addressed in this paper.",
            "mental-health_MAwBu4": "Not addressed in this paper."
        },
        {
            "papers_Knp69H": {
                "id": 2239856587,
                "title": "Correctional Mental Health",
