            "relevance_3LNXgq": "- **Relevance Score:** 65/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses prison mental\u2011health screening failures to highlight rights gaps; calls for systematic re\u2011evaluation and better care for incarcerated individuals.",
            "abstract_5B4jjq": "At sentencing, a judge may foresee that an individual with a major mental disorder will experience serious psychological or physical harm in prison. In light of this reality and offenders\u2019 other potential vulnerabilities, a number of jurisdictions currently allow judges to treat undue offender hardship as a mitigating factor at sentencing. In these jurisdictions, vulnerability to harm may militate toward an order of probation or a reduced term of confinement. Since these measures do not affect offenders\u2019 day-to-day experience in confinement, these expressions of mitigation fail to protect adequately those vulnerable offenders who must serve time in prison. This article argues that judges should possess the authority to tailor the conditions of vulnerable, disordered offenders\u2019 carceral sentences to ensure that sentences are humane, proportionate, and appropriate for serving the intended aims of punishment. To equalize, at least in part, conditions of confinement for this population, judges should consider ordering timely and periodic mental health evaluations by qualified professionals, disqualifying facilities with insufficient mental health or protective resources, specifying the facility or unit where an offender will serve or begin his sentence, and mandating certain treatment in prison. Allowing judges to exercise power over correctional conditions in this way will allow judges to fulfill better their institutional function of meting out appropriate, humane, and proportionate punishments, subject prison conditions to public scrutiny and debate, and help reform the image and reality of the criminal justice system for some of society\u2019s most vulnerable individuals.",
            "education-lev_LZfD4h": "Not addressed in this paper.",
            "mental-health_Bk3U8f": "Approximately 16% of prisoners have a mental disorder, exceeding community incidence (p. 3). Common conditions include schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, and depression (p. 6). Many prisons screen for mental health at intake, but shortcomings exist; 63% of inmates with acute mental symptoms were missed by routine jail screening in one study (p. 13).\n\nPrisons generally provide mental health care, including psychotropic medication (p. 17). However, 66% of state and 76% of federal prisoners with mental health problems received no treatment after admission (p. 13). Therapy is restricted, with 14 departments providing less than 1 hour per week for non-acute cases (p. 17). Staff often lack training to identify mental illness, misinterpreting symptoms as misconduct (p. 10, 14). Inmates may hide illnesses due to perceived adverse consequences (p. 13).",
            "prisoner-righ_Ulqd1Y": "Prisoners have an Eighth Amendment right to reasonably adequate medical care, extended to psychiatric care by circuit courts, and a Sixth Amendment right to appointed counsel at sentencing. They also have due process rights to present evidence and allocution. However, grievance procedures are not explicitly discussed.\n\nBarriers to exercising rights include inmates with serious mental disorders being unable to communicate accurately due to their illness or fear of adverse consequences like administrative lockdown. They often hide their illnesses. Correctional officers may misinterpret symptomatic illness as disorderly conduct.\n\nPrisoners in intermediate care programs desire to remain in the program for the duration of their sentences.",
            "prisoner-righ_UGCvQi": "U.S. prisoners possess legal rights, including the Eighth Amendment right to reasonably adequate medical care (Estelle v. Gamble). Indigent defendants have a Sixth Amendment right to appointed counsel at sentencing, and the right to allocution. States typically allow defense counsel to challenge or supplement presentence report information. However, the paper notes that mental health and vulnerability assessments are often conducted behind closed doors, limiting scrutiny. Correctional officials often rely on subjective judgments for protective custody. Prisoner councils or documented preferences/wish lists are not addressed.",
            "mental-health_MAwBu4": "Around 16% of U.S. prisoners have a mental disorder, exceeding community incidence. Axis I disorders include schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, and depression. Many prisons screen for mental health, but shortcomings lead to under-detection and unpredictable placements. 66% of state and 76% of federal prisoners with mental health problems received no treatment since admission. Mental health screens are often performed by non-psychologists, and one study found 63% of jail inmates with acute mental symptoms were missed."
        },
