            "relevance_3LNXgq": "- **Relevance Score:** 65/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses prison mental\u2011health service critique to highlight acute care gaps; discusses rights\u2011related service funding debates; mentions needs assessment without systematic data; lacks education or age\u2011entry analysis; no cross\u2011jurisdiction comparison.",
            "abstract_5B4jjq": "Psychiatrists have always been concerned about the mental health of prisoners If they did not devote much energy to their treatment, it was only because they had more-pressing problems, including how to squeeze ten patients into nine beds In any case, it was someone else\u2019s job to look after",
            "education-lev_LZfD4h": "Not addressed in this paper.",
            "mental-health_Bk3U8f": "Psychiatric services may not assess prisoners with mental illness brought to their notice. Only one-third of prisoners with known psychosis ended up in hospital, and the situation was worse for other diagnoses. Hard-pressed psychiatric services avoid admitting prisoners with mental illness due to higher difficulty, chronicity, and expense. Inreach services lead to community psychiatric nurses in prisons, but they may become disillusioned by difficulties in arranging assessment or admission from distant services. Many mentally disordered offenders do not need a hospital bed, suggesting a need for a wider range of psychiatric services within prisons. Efficient transfer of the acutely ill is a difficult question.",
            "prisoner-righ_Ulqd1Y": "The paper notes that psychiatric services may not assess prisoners with mental illness, and only a third of prisoners with known psychosis ended up in hospital, with worse outcomes for other diagnoses. It highlights that health care professionals cannot work efficiently if it's impossible to move acutely ill prisoners. The paper also discusses the logistical problems of transferring mentally ill prisoners and the potential for community psychiatric nurses to become disillusioned due to difficulties in arranging assessments or admissions from distant services. It does not directly address specific prisoner rights, representation, or expressed preferences.",
            "prisoner-righ_UGCvQi": "Not addressed in this paper.",
            "mental-health_MAwBu4": "Not addressed in this paper."
        },
        {
            "papers_Knp69H": {
                "id": 2607717217,
                "title": "Prisoners perceptions of a mental health wellness workshop",
                "is_archived": false,
                "full_slug": "prisoners-perceptions-of-a-mental-health-wellness-workshop-288z0hc6jy",
                "journal": {
                    "display_name": "International Journal of Prisoner Health",
                    "official_page": null,
                    "issn": "1744-9200",
                    "alias": null,
                    "id": 147041160,
                    "slug": "international-journal-of-prisoner-health",
                    "unique_id": "3t7epkok",
                    "full_slug": "international-journal-of-prisoner-health-3t7epkok"
                },
                "conference_series": null,
                "date": "2017-04-27 00:00:00+00:00",
                "is_oa": true,
                "publication_type": "Journal Article",
                "doi": "10.1108/IJPH-03-2016-0008",
                "unique_id": "288z0hc6jy",
                "abstract": "Purpose The purpose of this paper is to report on the evaluation of a one-day mental health wellness workshop which was delivered to male prisoners in an urban prison in the Republic of Ireland. Design/methodology/approach A mixed methods approach was used to evaluate the workshop. This paper presents the findings of the qualitative arm of the evaluation. Qualitative data were collected using semi-structured telephone interviews with ten participants who had completed the programme. Findings The participants were overwhelmingly positive about the wellness workshop and the qualitative interviews articulated the ways that the workshop impacted on their ability to manage their own and other peoples mental health. Originality/value As prisons attempt to limit the negative impact of prison life, implementing recovery orientated approaches such as the wellness workshop can have a positive impact on prisoners' mental health as well as raising their awareness and improving their attitudes towards mental distress and suicide. The concepts of self-help and peer support, espoused by the workshop offer a real opportunity to equip interested prisoners with skills to support themselves and other prisoners who are in distress.",
                "authors": {
                    "total": 6,
                    "data": [
                        {
                            "display_name": "Brian Keogh",
                            "id": 2115653365,
                            "slug": "brian-keogh",
                            "unique_id": "2uhjf3os4q",
                            "full_slug": "brian-keogh-2uhjf3os4q",
                            "affiliation_full_slug": "university-college-dublin-2uzykgj9"
                        },
                        {
