            "relevance_3LNXgq": "- **Relevance Score:** 62/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses narratives of incarcerated older Thai women to examine age\u2011specific pathways; provides voice but lacks focus on education, rights advocacy, or systematic mental\u2011health service analysis.",
            "abstract_5B4jjq": "In feminist criminology, there is a growing body of research exploring gendered pathways into prison. However, to date, this scholarship has not considered how age and gender may intersect to impact women\u2019s criminalization experiences. In this chapter, the authors have consequently chosen to explore older women\u2019s (aged 50+ years) narratives of their journeys to prison in Thailand using a feminist pathways approach. Results show several common threads in the stories of these women. Most were criminalized for the first time in later adulthood, had lived with various childhood and adulthood adversities, including, but not limited, to victimization and financial precarity, and had familial caretaking responsibilities. Many also recounted problems with substance misuse. Additionally, two relatively distinct pathways to prison emerged from the narratives: (1) economically motivated, (2) adversity, emotional distress, and addiction. A third pathway \u2013 intersectional, diffuse and unique \u2013 was also identified. It included themes from the first two pathways and the story of one woman that could not be categorized elsewhere. While the imprisonment pathways found mirrored those from previous pathways scholarship points of difference are noted. ",
            "education-lev_LZfD4h": "The research explored older women (aged 50+) in Thailand, finding most were criminalized for the first time in later adulthood. Pathways included economic precarity, childhood and adulthood adversities (victimization), familial caretaking, and substance misuse. Two distinct pathways emerged: economically motivated, and adversity, emotional distress, and addiction. A third, intersectional pathway was also identified. No specific data on educational attainment, literacy rates, high school completion, college education, learning disabilities, age at first arrest, or juvenile vs. adult entry is available in the metadata.",
