                "id": 4071236061,
                "title": "Human Rights of Prisoners",
                "is_archived": false,
                "full_slug": "human-rights-of-prisoners-ts8so0krjtxn",
                "journal": null,
                "conference_series": null,
                "date": "2025-07-03 00:00:00+00:00",
                "is_oa": null,
                "publication_type": "Journal Article",
                "doi": "10.4018/979-8-3373-1792-2.ch008",
                "unique_id": "ts8so0krjtxn",
                "abstract": "Prisoners' human rights are a significant topic in today's global talks. This is especially relevant when examining how they intersect with the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). Specifically, SDG 16 (Peace, Justice, and Strong Institutions) and SDG 3 (Good Health and Well-being). These two SDGs are crucial in ensuring that convicts are treated well and with dignity. This research explores how equitable justice systems can contribute to SDG 16 and SDG 3 progress. The United Nations Standard Minimum Rules for the Treatment of Prisoners, also known as the Nelson Mandela Rules, emphasize the need for humane treatment.This research paper examines how SDG 16 and SDG 3 are interconnected within the prison system. It emphasizes that developing the justice system is not only about ensuring fairness in legal proceedings. Additionally, the study calls for further reforms to address the drawbacks of global prison systems. It recommends research to examine the long-term effects of inclusive justice systems on prisoner rehabilitation and reintegration. ",
                "authors": {
                    "total": 1,
                    "data": [
                        {
                            "display_name": "Kanisha Pathak",
                            "full_slug": null
                        }
                    ]
