            },
            "relevance_3LNXgq": "- **Relevance Score:** 35/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Marginally Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses incarceration-based education programs to examine recidivism, directly addressing prisoners and their schooling outcomes. Lacks discussion of entry age, rights, or mental health; briefly notes access barriers and preferences.",
            "abstract_5B4jjq": "Higher education, GED programs, and vocational training play a major role in reducing recidivism among incarcerated individuals. Research shows that these educational initiatives inside incarceration not only lower recidivism rates but also support successful reintegration into society by increasing employment opportunities. This paper aims to analyze the effectiveness of each educational program and its direct impact on recidivism. Despite the effectiveness of each educational program in reducing recidivism rates among previously incarcerated individuals, challenges such as limited access, program quality, and post-release support persist. Recommendations for enhancing these program's effectiveness and impact include expanding access to higher education within correctional facilities, improving the delivery and integration of GED programs, increasing investment in vocational training aligned with labor market trends, developing holistic reentry programs that address various post-incarceration challenges, and standardizing recidivism definitions for more reliable research. Addressing these recommendations can strengthen the impact of correctional education programs, reduce recidivism, and promote successful reintegration, ultimately further contributing to increased public safety and economic stability. ",
            "education-lev_LZfD4h": "",
            "mental-health_Bk3U8f": "",
            "prisoner-righ_Ulqd1Y": "",
            "prisoner-righ_UGCvQi": "",
            "mental-health_MAwBu4": ""
        },
        {
            "papers_Knp69H": {
                "id": 1519571172,
                "title": "Pathways to Prison in New York State",
                "is_archived": false,
                "full_slug": "pathways-to-prison-in-new-york-state-v2o6fgsrcc",
                "journal": {
                    "display_name": "Criminology and public policy",
                    "official_page": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/17459133",
                    "issn": "1538-6473",
                    "alias": [
                        "Criminology and public policy"
                    ],
                    "id": 2764941010,
                    "slug": "criminology-and-public-policy",
                    "unique_id": "ya8kbec1",
                    "full_slug": "criminology-and-public-policy-ya8kbec1"
                },
                "conference_series": null,
                "date": "2015-08-01 00:00:00+00:00",
                "is_oa": false,
                "publication_type": "Journal Article",
                "doi": "10.1111/1745-9133.12136",
                "unique_id": "v2o6fgsrcc",
                "abstract": "Research Summary In this study, we use a novel application of group-based trajectory modeling to estimate pathways to prison for a sample of 13,769 first-time prison inmates in New York State. We found that 12% of the sample was heavily involved in the criminal justice system for 10 years prior to their first imprisonment. We also found that less than one quarter of the sample had little contact with the criminal justice system prior to the arrest that resulted in imprisonment. Policy Implications Slightly less than one quarter of first-time inmates are not known to the criminal justice system prior to the commitment arrest. For these inmates, crime-prevention interventions that identify participants through criminal justice processes will not be effective. However, the arrest rates for a substantial portion of the sample over the 10-year period before imprisonment suggest a staggering number of opportunities for intervention as these individuals churn through the system. Language: en",
                "authors": {
                    "total": 4,
                    "data": [
                        {
                            "display_name": "Sarah Tahamont",
