                },
                "tldr": "This paper examined how the rise in incarceration and its disproportionate concentration among low-skill, young African American men influences estimates of educational attainment in the United States and focused on high school graduation rates and the persistent gap in attainment that exists between young black and white Americans.",
                "podcast": null,
                "podcast_generation_allowed": true,
                "entity_type": "PAPER",
                "rank": 16,
                "relevance_data": {},
                "source": "scispace",
                "relevance_summary": "Uses incarceration data to reveal hidden high\u2011school dropout rates among black men, but does not examine prisoners' own education, ages of entry, rights, or mental health.",
                "relevance_metadata": {
                    "criteria_judgments": [
                        {
                            "criterion_name": "Incarcerated Population Focus",
                            "relevance": "Somewhat Relevant"
                        },
                        {
                            "criterion_name": "Educational Attainment of Prisoners",
                            "relevance": "Somewhat Relevant"
                        },
                        {
                            "criterion_name": "Age at Incarceration Entry and Pathways",
                            "relevance": "Not Relevant"
                        },
                        {
                            "criterion_name": "Prisoner Rights, Voice, and Advocacy",
                            "relevance": "Not Relevant"
                        },
                        {
                            "criterion_name": "Mental Health and Psychological Well\u2011Being of Prisoners",
                            "relevance": "Not Relevant"
                        }
                    ]
                }
            },
            "relevance_3LNXgq": "- **Relevance Score:** 20/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Not Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses incarceration data to reveal hidden high\u2011school dropout rates among black men, but does not examine prisoners' own education, ages of entry, rights, or mental health.",
            "abstract_5B4jjq": "This article examines how the rise in incarceration and its disproportionate concentration among low-skill, young African American men influences estimates of educational attainment in the United States. We focus on high school graduation rates and the persistent gap in attainment that exists between young black and white Americans. Although official statistics show a declining racial gap in high school dropout in recent years, conventional data sources exclude the incarcerated population from sample data. We show how those exclusions underestimate the extent of racial inequality in high school graduation and underestimate the dropout rate among young black men by as much as 40 percent. America\u2019s prisons and jails have become repositories for high school dropouts, thereby obscuring the degree of disadvantage faced by black men in the contemporary United States and the relative competitiveness of the U.S. workforce.",
            "education-lev_LZfD4h": "",
            "mental-health_Bk3U8f": "",
            "prisoner-righ_Ulqd1Y": "",
            "prisoner-righ_UGCvQi": "",
            "mental-health_MAwBu4": ""
        },
        {
            "papers_Knp69H": {
                "id": 2341211712,
                "title": "The Dimensions, Pathways, and Consequences of Youth Reentry",
                "is_archived": false,
                "full_slug": "the-dimensions-pathways-and-consequences-of-youth-reentry-5cuzxnagyc",
                "journal": null,
                "conference_series": null,
                "date": "2004-01-31 00:00:00+00:00",
                "is_oa": true,
                "publication_type": null,
                "doi": null,
                "unique_id": "5cuzxnagyc",
                "abstract": "Approximately 200,000 juveniles and young adults age 24 and under leave secure juvenile correctional facilities or state and federal prisons and return home each year--a process that we call youth reentry. The unprecedented growth in incarceration means that communities across the country increasingly must confront the challenges of integrating ever-growing numbers of young people who have been in adult prisons or prison-like settings operated by the juvenile justice system. Youth may face numerous obstacles, including family dysfunction, poverty, drug abuse, and inadequate education, treatment, and services, all of which may not only contribute to criminal behavior but also to their success during reentry in avoiding crime and becoming a contributing member of society. This report, which summarizes the insights of participants in the Urban Institute's Youth Reentry Roundtable, examines these issues and provides policy and research recommendations.",
                "authors": {
                    "total": 1,
                    "data": [
                        {
                            "display_name": "Daniel P. Mears",
                            "id": 1623915945,
                            "slug": "daniel-p-mears",
